meta {
  name: Lead Pull - Apply
  type: http
  seq: 21
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/meta/lead-pull/runs/{{leadPullRunId}}/apply?tenant_id={{tenantId}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: auth_token={{authToken}}
}

docs {
  QUEUES an Apply of the importable staged rows: verdict IN (new,
  phone_duplicate, email_duplicate).

  Returns 202 { run_id, status: 'apply_queued' } at once. The Apply runs on the
  lead-pull poller (it used to run inline and 504'd behind the gateway's 30s
  proxy timeout). Poll Lead-Pull-Get-Run: apply_queued -> applying -> applied,
  then read counts.apply { attempted, applied, already_synced, skipped, failed }
  and apply_summary (per-row applied_status counts — the whole-run truth after
  a resumed Apply).

  The two duplicate verdicts are importable ON PURPOSE. The canonical write path
  supersedes on a phone match and returns the existing lead on an email match,
  and EITHER WAY an ext.meta_leads row is written — which is exactly what stops
  those leads being re-fetched by every future pull. Dropping them would make
  each pull re-surface the same leads forever.

  Apply calls syncLeadToDatabase — the SAME function the live webhook calls —
  once per row, rather than writing leads itself. That is what makes campaign
  typing and pool routing apply automatically, and what makes this idempotent:
  the function re-checks ext.meta_leads.meta_lead_id before it does anything and
  AGAIN inside its transaction, AT APPLY TIME AGAINST LIVE DATA rather than
  against a staging snapshot that may be hours old.

  Regression checks:
    * DOUBLE-CLICK IT. The run flips completed -> apply_queued under a FOR UPDATE
      SKIP LOCKED claim, so the second request gets 409 rather than queueing the
      same rows twice.
    * A BIG RUN DOES NOT 504. Apply 500+ rows: this call still returns 202 in
      milliseconds and the run reaches `applied` on its own.
    * INTERRUPT IT. Restart meta-conversion-api mid-Apply: the reaper returns the
      run to `completed` with an "Apply was interrupted" error_text (NOT
      `failed`), and pressing Apply again finishes only the still-pending rows.
    * RE-APPLYING inserts nothing: rows already applied are no longer pending,
      and syncLeadToDatabase's own dedup returns already_synced for any lead the
      webhook ingested in the meantime.
    * applying a run that is not `completed`  -> 409 naming the actual status
    * applying an already-applied run          -> 409
    * a run id from tenant B, as tenant A      -> 404
    * caller below super_admin                 -> 403
    * ONE BAD ROW MUST NOT ABORT THE BATCH. Stage a lead with no phone value
      (syncLeadToDatabase THROWS on that, and on a non-numeric lead id): it must
      land applied_status='failed' with the message on the row, while every
      other lead still applies. Check the failed count, then the rows.
    * a test lead that somehow reached an importable verdict is still skipped —
      isMetaTestLead is exported by lead-sync.service.ts but NOT called inside
      syncLeadToDatabase, and the caller applying it is what stops Lead Ads
      Testing Tool submissions becoming real leads (20 of them once did).
    * an unmapped_form row (org_id NULL) is skipped with that reason and NEVER
      applied.
    * APPLIED LEADS CARRY ROUTING: check lms.marketing_leads.campaign_type_id
      and assigned_user_id on a newly applied lead — they must match what the
      webhook path would have produced, because the write went through the same
      canonical intake endpoint.
}
